Commentary of al-Nasafi (*Madarik al-Tanzil*) on surah 41, verse 5:

﴿And they say, "Our hearts are in coverings,"﴾ *aghtiya* (coverings), the plural of *kinan*, which means covering;

﴿from what you call us to,﴾ namely *al-tawhid* (the affirmation of God's oneness);

﴿and in our ears is a heaviness,﴾ *thiql* (a weight), which prevents us from hearing your speech;

﴿and between us and you is a barrier,﴾ *sitr* (a screen). These are figurative expressions for the hardening of their hearts against accepting the truth and believing in it, as though they were in sheaths and coverings that prevent it from penetrating them, and for their rejection of it with their ears, as though there were deafness to it in them. And because the two paths and the two religions were so far apart, it was as though between them and what they were upon, and between the Messenger of God (ﷺ) and what he was upon, there were a concealing barrier and an impregnable obstruction, like a mountain or the like, so that there was no meeting and no seeing of one another;

﴿So act,﴾ upon your religion;

﴿indeed we are acting,﴾ upon our religion. Or it means: act in nullifying our affair, and indeed we are acting in nullifying yours. The benefit of the added *min* (in *mimma* and *min baynina*) is that the barrier began from us and began from you, so the intervening space between our side and your side is entirely filled by the barrier, with no gap in it. If it had been said, "between us and you is a barrier," the meaning would have been that a barrier exists somewhere in the middle between the two sides.

Commentary of al-Nasafi (Madarik al-Tanzil) on surah 41, verse 46:

﴿Whoever does righteousness, it is for his own self﴾, meaning he benefits himself;

﴿and whoever does evil, it is against it﴾, meaning he harms himself;

﴿and your Lord is not ever unjust to the servants﴾, so that He would punish one who is not an evildoer.
